## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures, promises, and memoization in a practical frontend pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and encapsulation
- In-flight deduping of promises
- TTL caching and cleanup
- ES6 features: Map, arrow functions, async/await
- Testing approaches for async caches

## Code Example
```javascript
function createDataLoader(fetchFn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const TTL = 60_000;
  const now = () => Date.now();
  function cleanup() {
    const t = now();
    for (const [k, v] of cache) {
      if (t - v.ts >= TTL) cache.delete(k);
    }
  }
  setInterval(cleanup, TTL);
  return {
    load(id) {
      const entry = cache.get(id);
      if (entry && (now() - entry.ts) < TTL) return entry.promise;
      const p = Promise.resolve(fetchFn(id)).finally(() => {});
      cache.set(id, { promise: p, ts: now() });
      return p;
    },
    clear(id){ cache.delete(id); },
    clearAll(){ cache.clear(); }
  };
}

// usage
const loader = createDataLoader((id)=> fetch(`/api/user/${id}`).then(r=>r.json()));
loader.load(123).then(console.log);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL expiry and concurrent loads?
- How would you adapt for per-resource invalidation or gc tuning?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests integration of fetch, WebSocket, Service Worker, and offline persistence in a realistic UI while focusing on DOM efficiency.

## Key Concepts

- Ordered patch application with per-patch IDs and timestamps
- Service Worker caching for REST data and assets with stale-while-revalidate
- WebSocket reconnect strategy and backoff
- IndexedDB as an offline queue with idempotent replay and conflict checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPatches(root, patches){
  // patches: [{id, type, selector, value, ts}]
  patches.sort((a,b)=> a.id - b.id);
  for(const p of patches){
    // minimal DOM update logic depending on type
    const el = root.querySelector(p.selector);
    if(!el) continue;
    switch(p.type){
      case 'update': el.textContent = p.value; break;
      case 'move': el.style.transform = `translate(${p.value.x}px, ${p.value.y}px)`; break;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test patch ordering under reconnect bursts?
- How would you handle patch duplication or lost patches in edge cases?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to manage per-key caching, in-flight request deduplication, and request cancellation using closures and promises in a real-world data-loading module.

## Key Concepts
- Closure-based per-key state management
- In-flight request deduplication and cancellation with AbortController
- TTL-based cache invalidation
- Async/await error handling and race condition management

## Code Example
```javascript
function createLoader() {
  const cache = new Map();
  return async function load(query, page) {
    const key = JSON.stringify({ query, page });
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = cache.get(key);
    if (cached && now - cached.timestamp < 5 * 60 * 1000) {
      return cached.data;
    }
    if (cached?.controller) {
      cached.controller.abort();
    }
    const controller = new AbortController();
    cache.set(key, { controller, timestamp: now });
    try {
      const response = await fetch(`/api/items?query=${query}&page=${page}`, { signal: controller.signal });
      const data = await response.json();
      cache.set(key, { data, timestamp: now });
      return data;
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') throw err;
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design high-frequency data handling in React with Redux and Context, focusing on minimizing re-renders during bursts while supporting multiple views. It probes knowledge of selectors, memoization, and rendering strategies in real-world data load scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Memoized selectors (reselect) for derived data
- React.memo for pure chart components
- useTransition to defer updates during bursts
- Virtualization/windowing to handle large bucket lists
- Redux data flow with high-rate streams and separate UI state in Context

## Code Example

```javascript
// Memoized selector sketch (conceptual)
import { createSelector } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const selectEvents = (state) => state.metrics.events; // raw events stream

const bucketizeBySecond = createSelector(
  [selectEvents],
  (events) => {
    const buckets = [];
    let current = [];
    let currentSecond = null;
    for (const e of events) {
      const t = Math.floor(e.ts / 1000) * 1000;
      if (currentSecond !== t) {
        if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
        current = [e];
        currentSecond = t;
      } else {
        current.push(e);
      }
    }
    if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
    return buckets;
  }
);
```

